  "pr_number": 5540,
  "issue_number": 5538,
  "issue_title": "fix: MCP's normalizePlatform mirrors leak raw unrecognized input instead of returning empty like the registry package",
  "issue_body": "## Goal\n`packages/mcp`'s two duplicated `normalizePlatform`/`normalizeRegistryPlatform` implementations should return an empty value for unrecognized platform input, matching the canonical `packages/registry/src/platforms-lib.js#normalizePlatform` contract, instead of leaking the raw unrecognized string back out.\n\n## Why this matters\nThe registry package's `normalizePlatform` returns `undefined` for any value not in `PLATFORM_ALIASES` \u2014 callers treat that as \"no valid platform, don't filter.\" The MCP package has two independent mirrors of this function (`packages/mcp/src/registry-normalize-lib.js#normalizePlatform` and `packages/mcp/src/search-ranking-lib.js#normalizeRegistryPlatform`) that both fall back to `String(value || \"\").trim()` instead of an empty/undefined value, so an unrecognized platform string is returned verbatim rather than treated as \"unknown.\"\n\nThis directly breaks platform filtering: `matchesRegistryPlatform` (`search-ranking-lib.js`) does `const target = normalizeRegistryPlatform(platform); if (!target) return true;` \u2014 the intended behavior for an invalid/misspelled platform is \"no filter, match everything,\" but because the fallback is never falsy for non-empty input, a typo'd or garbage platform string becomes a strict-equality filter against canonical `entry.platforms` values it can never match. Every MCP tool that filters by platform (`search_registry`, `plan_workflow_toolbox`, compare/trust-compare/safety-review handlers in `registry-tool-orchestration-lib.js`) silently returns zero results for a misspelled platform instead of ignoring the bad filter, and echoes the garbage string back in tool responses as if it were an accepted platform value.\n\nVerified with a direct repro:\n```\nnode -e \"console.log(require('./packages/registry/src/platforms-lib.js').normalizePlatform('Cluade Code'))\"\n// undefined\nnode -e \"console.log(require('./packages/mcp/src/registry-normalize-lib.js').normalizePlatform('Cluade Code'))\"\n// \"Cluade Code\"  <- leaked verbatim\n```\n\n## Current behavior\n`packages/mcp/src/registry-normalize-lib.js` (`normalizePlatform`, ~line 51-55):\n```js\nexport function normalizePlatform(value) {\n  const normalized = normalizeText(value).replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g, \"-\");\n  if (!normalized) return \"\";\n  return platformAliases.get(normalized) || String(value || \"\").trim();\n}\n```\n`packages/mcp/src/search-ranking-lib.js` (`normalizeRegistryPlatform`, ~line 340-344) has the identical bug:\n```js\nexport function normalizeRegistryPlatform(value) {\n  const normalized = text(value).replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g, \"-\");\n  if (!normalized) return \"\";\n  return PLATFORM_ALIASES.get(normalized) || String(value || \"\").trim();\n}\n```\nCompare `packages/registry/src/platforms-lib.js` (`normalizePlatform`, ~line 72-78):\n```js\nexport function normalizePlatform(value) {\n  if (typeof value !== \"string\") return undefined;\n  const key = value.trim().toLowerCase();\n  return Object.hasOwn(PLATFORM_ALIASES, key) ? PLATFORM_ALIASES[key] : undefined;\n}\n```\n\n## Desired behavior\nBoth MCP-side functions return an empty string (matching their own existing empty-input branch, `if (!normalized) return \"\";`) when the input doesn't match a known alias, instead of falling back to the raw trimmed input. `matchesRegistryPlatform`'s `if (!target) return true` then correctly treats an unrecognized platform as \"no filter\" rather than an unmatchable strict filter.\n\n## Scope\n- `packages/mcp/src/registry-normalize-lib.js` \u2014 `normalizePlatform` only.\n- `packages/mcp/src/search-ranking-lib.js` \u2014 `normalizeRegistryPlatform` only.\n- focused tests in `tests/mcp-registry-normalize-lib.test.ts` and `tests/mcp-search-ranking-lib.test.ts`.\n\n## Out of scope\n- `packages/registry/src/platforms-lib.js` \u2014 already correct, reference only.\n- Any other function in either file (`normalizeText`, `normalizeLimit`, `normalizeOffset`, tokenization/search-matching functions unrelated to platform normalization).\n- Changing why two separate mirrors of this logic exist in the MCP package (that's a pre-existing dependency-isolation choice, not this issue's concern).\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- PR includes `Closes #<issue>`.\n- `normalizePlatform(\"Cluade Code\")` and `normalizeRegistryPlatform(\"Cluade Code\")` both return `\"\"`, not the raw input.\n- Both functions still return the canonical alias (e.g. `\"claude-code\"`) for known aliases, unchanged from today.\n- `matchesRegistryPlatform(entry, \"Cluade Code\")` returns `true` (no filter applied) instead of filtering out all entries.\n- New/updated unit tests in both affected test files cover at least one unrecognized-platform case.\n\n## Quality evidence required in the PR\nNo visual impact \u2014 this is an MCP tool/library correctness fix. List which MCP tools consume `normalizePlatform`/`normalizeRegistryPlatform` (via `matchesRegistryPlatform`) and confirm known-platform filtering behavior is unchanged, only the unrecognized-input path is fixed.\n\n## Validation\n```\npnpm exec vitest run tests/mcp-registry-normalize-lib.test.ts tests/mcp-search-ranking-lib.test.ts\npnpm test:mcp\npnpm validate:mcp-package\n```\n",
